# gorget-js snag #15f (2026-08-10, MED severity) — `gg fmt` rewrites
# every hex integer literal to decimal, destroying byte-idiom
# readability in lexer/parser code.
#
# RED signature:
#     int backslash = 0x5C          # backslash byte
#     if backslash == 0x5C:  # ...
#         ↓ gg fmt
#     int backslash = 92
#     if backslash == 92:  # backslash byte     ← comment now cryptic
#
# Behavior-preserving (value unchanged) but destroys the readable
# idiom every byte-manipulation surface relies on: `== 0x5C  #
# backslash`, `== 0x22  # quote`, `== 0x0A  # newline`. Rewriting
# to `== 92`/`== 34`/`== 10` desyncs the source from its comments
# and forces every reader to convert in their head.
#
# ~306 rewrite sites in gorget-js alone (lexer 129→2, eval 127→1,
# parser 39→0, abstract_ops 14→0). Formatters must preserve
# numeric radix — rustfmt/black/prettier all preserve the author's
# 0x-vs-decimal choice.
#
# INTENDED behavior: fmt output preserves the exact lexeme of every
# integer literal (0x prefix + digits, or decimal digits, as authored).
# Same for binary `0b...` and octal `0o...` if the lexer accepts them.
void main():
    int backslash = 0x5C
    int quote = 0x22
    int newline = 0x0A
    if backslash == 0x5C:
        print("bs")
    if quote == 0x22:
        print("q")
    if newline == 0x0A:
        print("nl")
